      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite a LinkedIn post of up to 500 words about a lesson learned from shipping a feature that nobody used.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nThe feature shipped on time. Nobody used it.\n\nWe’d done the work: polished the interface, handled edge cases, written documentation, and celebrated the release.\n\nThen we watched the dashboard.\n\nAlmost nothing.\n\nOur first instinct was to blame discoverability. Maybe it needed a better announcement. A tooltip. A more prominent button.\n\nBut after talking to customers, we found a harder truth:\n\nWe had validated that the idea sounded useful—not that the problem mattered enough for someone to change their behavior.\n\n“Would you use this?” had produced encouraging answers.\n\n“What do you do today when this happens?” revealed workarounds people were perfectly happy with.\n\nAnd “When was the last time this caused a real problem?” often led to a long pause.\n\nThe lesson wasn’t “never build something new.” It was this:\n\n**Interest is not intent. Shipping is not adoption.**\n\nSince then, I’ve tried to make three things clear before committing to a feature:\n\n• **The behavior we expect to change.** Not “users will have more flexibility,” but “users will stop exporting this report to a spreadsheet every Friday.”\n\n• **The evidence that the problem matters.** Recent examples, repeated workarounds, and time or money lost—not just positive reactions to a mockup.\n\n• **The smallest test that could prove us wrong.** A manual service, a rough prototype, or a limited pilot before a full build.\n\nWe also define what meaningful adoption would look like—and when we’ll review it. Otherwise, an unused feature can linger indefinitely because nobody wants to call it.\n\nI’m still proud of how that team delivered. But we delivered a good answer to a question our customers weren’t really asking.\n\nNow, I’d rather learn that in a week of discovery than a quarter of development.\n\nWhat’s a feature you shipped that changed how you decide what to build next?\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nI spent 6 weeks shipping a feature our users begged for.\n\nCustom dashboards. Drag-and-drop widgets. Export to PDF. Beautiful, fast, on time.\n\nLaunch day: 🎉\n\nWeek 1: 3% adoption.\nWeek 4: basically zero.\n\nI was crushed. Then I got curious and called 15 of the users who'd requested it.\n\nHere's what I learned:\n\n**1. They didn't want dashboards. They wanted answers.**\nWhat they really meant by \"custom dashboards\" was \"tell me if we're on track on Monday morning without digging.\" We built a power tool. They needed a 30-second email digest.\n\n**2. \"Everyone wants it\" meant 3 loud voices.**\nWe'd confused feedback volume with feedback breadth. We never validated with data, prototypes, or the silence from the other 97%.\n\n**3. We shipped output, not outcome.**\nSuccess was \"launch on time,\" not \"solve the problem weekly.\" We celebrated merging PRs instead of changing behavior.\n\nNow I live by this: Fall in love with the problem, not your solution.\n\nBefore I build anything, I ask:\n→ What's the job to be done in the user's words?\n→ How will we know it's working 30 days after launch?\n→ What's the smallest version that could deliver value?\n\nThat failed feature? We replaced it with a simple Monday Slack summary. 68% weekly open rate. Took 4 days to build.\n\nSometimes the best product work is unbuilding your assumptions.\n\nWhat's a feature YOU shipped that nobody used — and what did it teach you? 👇\n\n#ProductManagement #Startups #UX #Leadership #LessonsLearned\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
